WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.680
Almost two years after the&nbsp;
slap heard 'round the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.760
Jada Pinkett Smith is speaking out about both&nbsp;
the incident and her marriage to Will Smith.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.640
While promoting her new book "Worthy," which&nbsp;
hits shelves on October 17, Pinkett Smith sat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.760
down with People to reveal something huge about&nbsp;
the viral Oscars moment where Will Smith slapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.680
presenter Chris Rock after Rock made a joke&nbsp;
about Pinkett Smith's shaved head. Pinkett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:29.120
Smith lives with alopecia and chooses to keep&nbsp;
her head shaved as a result. Needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.160
a "G.I. Jane" joke made by Rock didn't go&nbsp;
over well. Smith famously followed up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.000
slap by telling Rock, quote, "Keep my&nbsp;
wife's name out your f---ing mouth."

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.680
Accord to Pinkett Smith, she simply assumed&nbsp;
the situation was a planned bit ... and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.840
just as shocked as anybody to discover that Smith&nbsp;
actually slapped Rock in earnest. She told People,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.560
"I thought, 'This is a skit. I was like,&nbsp;
'There's no way that Will hit him.' It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.960
wasn't until Will started to walk back to his&nbsp;
chair that I even realized it wasn't a skit."

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.840
The moment was definitely shocking, and&nbsp;
it led to huge consequences for Smith — so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.480
it's an added surprise to hear that Pinkett&nbsp;
Smith was apparently baffled by it too. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.520
also revealed that the first thing she asked Smith&nbsp;
following the ceremony was simply, "Are you okay?"

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.840
That moment when Will Smith slapped Chris Rock&nbsp;
back in March 2022 was incredibly confusing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.600
first — in fact, several feeds of the ceremony&nbsp;
went completely blank as networks panicked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.680
But when the dust settled, all hell really&nbsp;
broke loose. Shortly after slapping Rock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.080
Smith got up on the stage to accept his award&nbsp;
for best actor for his role in "King Richard,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.960
a film about Venus and Serena Williams' father&nbsp;
Richard Williams. The anointed winner then gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.600
an extraordinarily tearful speech and&nbsp;
received a standing ovation. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.800
Smith started facing major&nbsp;
consequences almost immediately.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.840
"When I open my eyes I didn't realize, I&nbsp;
was like 'Why is everyone so quiet?' Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.400
I literally was not present&nbsp;
for that whole entire moment."

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.280
Not only was there reportedly an argument&nbsp;
over whether or not to kick Smith out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.640
the ceremony on the spot, but Smith also&nbsp;
resigned from the Academy (he'd been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.440
member since 2001) and was eventually&nbsp;
banned from the ceremony for ten full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.880
years. In response, Smith released&nbsp;
a simple, brief statement that said,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.720
"I accept and respect the Academy's decision."

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.800
Smith's next movie, "Emancipation," barely made a&nbsp;
splash when it came out in 2022, and a sequel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.920
his Netflix original movie "Bright" was canceled.&nbsp;
Smith may have walked away from the ceremony with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.520
a statuette for best actor, but the effect this&nbsp;
slap had on his career still feels irreversible.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.600
Rock, meanwhile, took some time to comment&nbsp;
on the slap but ultimately addressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.497
the incident in a Netflix stand-up&nbsp;
special titled "Selective Outrage."

00:02:43.497 --> 00:02:51.960
"People are like, 'Did it hurt?' It still hurts!&nbsp;
I've got 'Summertime' ringing in my ears."

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.080
Jada Pinkett Smith had one last bombshell&nbsp;
to drop in her People interview. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.240
though Will Smith implored Chris Rock to&nbsp;
keep his "wife's" name out of his mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.200
the couple has actually been separated&nbsp;
since 2016. Pinkett Smith told People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.240
after revealing that the two were&nbsp;
long separated at the 2022 Oscars,

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.840
"We're still figuring it out. We've been doing&nbsp;
some really heavy-duty work together. We just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.120
got deep love for each other and we are going&nbsp;
to figure out what that looks like for us."

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.720
With that said, she also clarified that&nbsp;
she and Smith are there for one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.560
as much as they can be. Regarding the slap&nbsp;
and its ramifications, Pinkett Smith said,

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.280
"I'm going to be by his side but also allow&nbsp;
him to have to figure this out for himself."

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.440
Pinkett Smith's memoir, which might&nbsp;
give people even more insight into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.683
her marriage and the infamous&nbsp;
slap, comes out on October 17.

